Chris difficult day just talked us  through that incident that everyone's  talking about  yeah tough day out there I mean  obviously from a from a riding side of  things it was a great day for us another  day we can take off and I mean I was  tested by nairo quintana in the final  there but I felt quite within myself  able to respond and just happily sort of  keep my position on the general  classification unfortunately there was  there was an incident out on the road  which yeah it doesn't doesn't leave us  with a bit of good feeling a fan of  supposed fan at one point on one of the  drags quite early on in the stage  through a plots of urine at me while  shouting dope a like that  so it was it was clearly a French fan  and that it's not acceptable it's not  acceptable on any level obviously we're  all doing our level best out here to try  and try and race it as hard as we can to  try and change the image of the sport  that's that's obviously really important  to us and obviously this guy's got the  wrong end of the stick and he's taking  it out on us I mean we all want a clean  sport unfortunately I feel as if a lot  of the the reporting on the race has  been very irresponsible in terms of oh  can we trust this can we trust this  performance and that's that's obviously  led to a lot of people out on the roads  booing us and acting in this kind of  manner but I mean having said that it is  the minority it is absolute minority I'd  say it's it's it's a couple percent of  the thousands upon thousands of people  who are out there supporting us and and  cheering for all the riders so it's it's  a shame that one or two idiots can can  basically ruin it for Europe given that  incident do you worry about your  security now you're very exposed and we  are very exposed on the road  security is always a bit of a concern  especially when you've got that sort of  mob mentality in groups but we're in the  race organizers hands for that and we  hope that obviously they're going to do  everything they can to to make sure  we're not in a hostile environment out  on the road what's your message to  French cycling fans who don't believe  you are clean and are shouting abuse at  you and throwing urine at you you've had  a team mates spat on today another  teammate was punched earlier in the week  here what's your message to these people  my message to them would be try and make  up your own mind about things don't  listen to the likes of certain  commentators who are going to they're  from the old era these are ex cyclists  there's they're the ones saying listen  this performance looks dubious this this  doesn't look right to me and make up  your own mind you look at the race look  at how hard we work to get here we're  not we're not cheating and only only  time will tell  but it's it's a shame if we're going to  have to wait 10 15 years to really be  credited with the victories that we've  got here do you find it ironic that  you're the one cyclist who is  consistently open and honest you've had  journalists embedded with you on the  tour and you are repeatedly calling for  more testing that you're the one guy  that's asking for more and yet people  are abusing it certainly I mean for me  it's really important to be a spokesman  for clean cycling I mean it's a I feel  as if it's a it is something that falls  on my shoulders now to try and change  the image of the past and and obviously  the the Tour winners that came before me  who through the image of cycling out the  window  so I do feel a large responsibility and  I'm trying my level best to to try and  rectify that but it obviously doesn't  help when we've got these people on the  sidelines doing doing things like today  you've even said at the end of this tour  you will happily go to an independent  panel of doctors let them look at you I  just correct you there I wouldn't  necessarily say doctors I mean we're in  the middle of the race my focus is here  on the Tour de France but I am  open-minded to going and getting some  testing done not necessarily blood tests  or anything like that but oh it depends  what what people wants one one to see  but I'm thinking more physiological  testing something along those lines to  to maybe explain why my performances are  the way they are why I'm able to do what  I'm able to do I don't know if you'll  find any answers that's I'm certainly  open to it yeah and tell me how much  does it hurt them to have all the  questions and the abuse and the kind of  all the you know conspiracy theories why  is he able to do this it's it's  frustrating I won't lie it is  frustrating but at the same time it's  it's not gonna throw my focus off I'm  here to race I'm here to win  and that's it's not gonna change my  focus the same time it does it is  frustrating how that's that's all I can  say about it is it's a shame it has to  come with that same level of scrutiny  that that that we've got here at the  tour does that incident spory you want I  certainly I mean I think is it as a  group as a team I think it's it's  brought us all together in a way we all  feel as if like come on that's that's  let's dig in here let's let's do this I  mean come on everyone's we're up against  it here but you know what we've trained  pretty hard to be here excuse my French  we're not gonna let a few idiots ruin it  for us your one week away from Paris no  no British rider has ever won this race  twice how much of an incentive is that  to write history that's that's not  really what I'm about to try and write  history and but I mean obviously I want  to be the best sportsman I can be I want  to I want to make my family proud I want  to make my friends proud I want to make  all the people who've got me here my  mentors my coaches I want to make them  proud I've got I've put a little kid on  the way I'd love to be able to say to  him one day look what your dad did when  when you were in your mom's tummy those  those are my motivations fatherhood when  the time comes if your little boy says  to your daddy I'd love to be a  professional cyclist given you know how  hard it is it's excruciating at times  and yet immensely pleasurable yeah would  you be happy for your son to become a  cyclist one day I definitely wouldn't  discourage him from it it's it's a  beautiful sport I mean I I love the  sport through and through otherwise I  certainly wouldn't be doing what I'm  doing now yeah certainly I mean I'd be  pretty proud to have have a little one  on a bike in a few years time tell me  about the guys in Team Sky they killed  themselves most days you know for you  yeah and the spirits the camaraderie the  unity what's it like inside that truck  with especially this year we really do  have a tight-knit group of guys who it  seems as if everyone's got good days and  bad days  some guys struggling on on certain  stages and we're all just for changing  roles within the team and just getting  through each day one day at a time  trying to just get oh that they're just  trying to carry me to the finish  obviously and invest in the best best  way possible and it's it's been  fantastic the guys have done such a good  job like the job up until now I mean if  we look at all the hurdles we've we've  had in that first week we had torrential  thunderstorms with cross  and the cobbled stages but all the way  through the guys have been there and  right up into through the Pyrenees I've  always had either Garant on us or Richie  bought with me I can't I can't thank the  guys enough for the work they've done  for me here people back home are saying  that Chris Froome 2015 is the mentally  stronger that that they've seen you  where where does that come from you know  we see you on the television we see you  in pain and yes you dig it out time and  again when you're attacked people are  coming at you where do you get your  mental strength from um I mean I  definitely feel as if from a lot more  prepared this time around for it and  then I was two years ago I felt like two  years ago it was still very new to me it  was sort of a big scary experience  whereas this time round I feel as if  okay I know what I'm up against here  right I've done this before there's no  reason why I can't do it again but I  mean at the end of the day this is this  is by creating this is sport I mean we  should be enjoying it and we are it's  mature to be in this position  I asked Sir Dave Brailsford earlier on a  couple of questions about some of the  kind of internet conspiracy theories out  there about Team Sky yeah he said they  are hilarious so I'm gonna ask you to  your face now people say that you have  some sort of hidden motor device inside  your bicycle that drives you on yeah  I've heard it all on this racing motors  to all of its I mean in a way it's got  to be the greatest compliment that  people think I've got a motor in my bike  but I mean I don't really take it like  that obviously it's it's it does get  irritating and it's it's silly I mean  the authorities are checking our bikes  for motors just for the record  but yeah I mean it's it is crazy some of  the stuff you read out there but I guess  sort of thought always be conspiracy  theorists out there people say you drink  an energy drink that cost 2,000 pounds a  liter is this yeah that's that's another  one actually has a quote that made us  laugh we had to actually get on to get  onto internet and google the name of the  drink to see exactly what it was but now  there's certainly no 2000 euro Alita  drinks happening on TV sky all motors or  anything like that the next week looking  ahead it it's a week that defines your  year and your in your career yeah and  how do you switch off in the evenings  you know knowing how big every day is  that's coming do you do you relax do you  read do you ring Michelle oh yeah I was  try and try and give the wife a call  give Michelle a call  that's always nice just to hear what's  happening at home put some music on this  to be honest is not too much time in the  evenings once we finish for dinner you  pretty much want to hit the hit the sack  and try and get some rest before before  you're up early the next day for for  another big stage so there's not a lot  of downtime so what little downtime we  have we try and try and keep in touch  with family to all the legions of fans  back home who are following your  progress and who loved what you were  doing for the sport and they believed in  you and they love that you were riding  clean and they support you all the way  what's your message to them I mean the  support we've had from back home is just  unreal just the thousands upon thousands  of messages of Union Jack's out on the  roads of people who have actually made  it their mission to come out here and  follow us day by day holding whatever  banner or support support message it's  it's unreal it's unreal and I just want  to thank those people from the bottom of  my heart thank you so much for the sport  and it it really means a lot to us and  it gets us through the stages just  looking ahead to the next few days and  heading into Paris hopefully with you in  a yellow jersey yeah what does it feel  like you've done it once before what to  feel like when protocol dictates that  you don't get attacked on the on the  right into Paris and Sean's alizée and  you know you're there I almost feel a  little bit too superstitious to be  talking about that just yet I mean it  was still good a week to go and I mean I  am in a fantastic position with over  three minutes advantage to to second  place and of course every day I'm  thinking about what its gonna be like to  to write down that Sean's at lycée and  be in that kind of position but I'm not  gonna let myself get too carried away  just yet I've got to get there first I  met a man two years ago in Kenya who was  your old mentor David Kinjo truly a  remarkable man how much of an influence  was he on you two how he learned how you  rolled to begin with and he gave you  belief definitely definitely in kyndra  kyndra was a huge inspiration to me when  when I was a teenager back in Kenya and  it was just amazing to me that he was a  professional at the time and he just  said to me come on get your bike come on  let's let's go training and to me it  just felt felt amazing that you could  you could connect with people like that  and experience the outdoors and in such  a way it was it was just for me it was  freedom as a teenager on a bike and  kynges inspired a lot of youngsters it's  been sad that that more haven't been  able to to move away from from Kenya on  to the professional scene but he's doing  a great job back in Kenya now motivating  youngsters and working working with with  an Academy they're so  no they definitely definitely keep up  the good work and you there's one final  question the next few days  you must really hope you don't get the  level of aggression and hostility and  abuse that you've had today certainly  certainly I mean I'd like to think  that's that's hopefully behind us now  and having drawn attention to it these  last few days with with the incidents  that have happened hopefully it's going  to die down a bit now but who knows I  mean it's to the front and anything  could happen out here so your message to  everyone that doubts you Chris Froome  riding clean winning clean and like I  said before time time will tell but only  only I know that and unfortunately other  people are going to have to wait a bit  longer to see that for themselves
 

